Rule 1 (inputs must have numeric, boolean, or complex dtypes; strings and dtype objects are disallowed)
{v_1 : tensor, v_2 : tensor} |= (0 ≤ dtype_(v_1) ∧ dtype_(v_1) ≤ 10) ∧ (0 ≤ dtype_(v_2) ∧ dtype_(v_2) ≤ 10)
>>
Rule 2 (each tensor’s dimensions are non-negative)
{v_1 : tensor, v_2 : tensor} |= (∀i ∈ [0, ndim(v_1) - 1] : shape(v_1, i) ≥ 0) ∧ (∀j ∈ [0, ndim(v_2) - 1] : shape(v_2, j) ≥ 0)
>>
Rule 3 (string and dtype-object dtypes are explicitly forbidden)
{v_1 : tensor, v_2 : tensor} |= if (dtype_(v_1) = 11 ∨ dtype_(v_2) = 11 ∨ dtype_(v_1) = 12 ∨ dtype_(v_2) = 12) then false
>>
Rule 4 (if either input is complex, both inputs still must be non-string/non-dtype)
{v_1 : tensor, v_2 : tensor} |= if (dtype_(v_1) = 9 ∨ dtype_(v_1) = 10 ∨ dtype_(v_2) = 9 ∨ dtype_(v_2) = 10) then ((0 ≤ dtype_(v_1) ∧ dtype_(v_1) ≤ 10) ∧ (0 ≤ dtype_(v_2) ∧ dtype_(v_2) ≤ 10))
>>
Rule 5 (for 2-D inputs, pairwise dimension products are non-negative)
{v_1 : tensor, v_2 : tensor} |= if (ndim(v_1) = 2 ∧ ndim(v_2) = 2) then ((shape(v_1, 0) * shape(v_2, 0) ≥ 0) ∧ (shape(v_1, 1) * shape(v_2, 1) ≥ 0))
>>
Rule 6 (if either input is a scalar, only dtype validity is required)
{v_1 : tensor, v_2 : tensor} |= if (ndim(v_1) = 0 ∨ ndim(v_2) = 0) then ((0 ≤ dtype_(v_1) ∧ dtype_(v_1) ≤ 10) ∧ (0 ≤ dtype_(v_2) ∧ dtype_(v_2) ≤ 10))
